WEBVTT

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:07.650
 It came a lot from Dennis Lee, his family. They gave us different seeds to start, like the

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:13.090
 mustards, the chilies, the perilla. We just knew we had to save our own seeds in the beginning,

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:18.430
 and then started refining that as we went. People just responded so quickly to see something

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:23.290
 that was really explicitly about Korean food. And so suddenly people were just like giving

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:27.950
 me seeds that were from their family, or seeds that they wanted a little bit of access to.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.310
 They're like, "Oh, you can grow this. Can you just give me a little?" And so it just sort

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.750
 of evolved from there where suddenly we became this clearinghouse for people to just like

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:40.610
 send us seeds because, you know, they really believe in sort of like the food politics of

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:46.710
 somewhere like the Bay, but we're limited in having access to like Korean vegetables grown

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:53.050
 organically. And so they were constantly having to like choose and prioritize like, do we want

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.360
 to like omit these really significant things and these like things we want our kids to grow

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.580
 up eating, or do we want to prioritize general health and well-being?

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:13.070
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:18.550
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:24.120
 scientists. These people have shaped the food movement in California. We talk with a diverse

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:29.720
 group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens,

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.960
 and communities that reshape the way we think about food. This show is made by Devin Sampson

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.360
 and Chelsea Wills. Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:46.750
 Murillo for making this season possible. Kristen Leach runs Namu Farm at the Sanol Ag Park east

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:52.510
 of the San Francisco Bay. She grows vegetables for San Francisco restaurant Namu Gaji and seeds

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:58.730
 for the Kitazawa Seed Company. Kristen grows both traditional and whimsical produce, focusing

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:05.310
 on Korean varieties and using organic, biodynamic, and permacultural practices without any fossil fuels.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:10.710
 She discovered the foods of her Korean heritage further on in her life as she was adopted as

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:18.050
 as an infant and grew up in New York, where she later became a part of the urban gardening movement.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:29.220
 Thanks so much for taking the time in the middle of a bunch of harvests to be on Delicious Revolution.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:29.600
 Oh, thank you.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.400
 And thanks for the farm tour. That was totally amazing. We just saw an incredible diversity of

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:44.770
 lots of plants I had never even heard of. So yeah, so thanks so much. I guess I wanted to

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:50.210
 start by talking about what's the food of your childhood? What kind of food did you grow up eating?

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:59.110
 Uh, um, I think just sort of standard American food, what you could expect in sort of a typical

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:08.460
 household. It was just, um, yeah, pretty basic. Like, I definitely ate a lot of vegetables and,

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:15.070
 um, things that like sometimes me or my brothers could prepare at home by ourselves. Um, but we

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:20.530
 definitely like sat down and ate dinner and, you know, like my parents put forward some effort. They

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:25.790
 both worked, but, you know, made an effort that at least one of them would be there to kind of make

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:35.220
 something. And, um, yeah, but typical, you know, hamburgers, sandwiches, like just chicken. And, um, my dad

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:40.180
 worked as a caterer for a little while. Um, and so sometimes he would bring home stuff that felt like

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:46.110
 really fancy, but looking back, it's kind of awful. Like Hawaiian chicken with pineapple slices and

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:53.600
 maraschino cherries. And, um, but I appreciate they were really resourceful. Yeah. I loved food as a

00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:00.820
 kid. So even just basic things like, you know, when I was a teenager, I started doing food, not bombs in

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:08.740
 New York and, um, you know, started having these certain ideas about food and its significance. And so I

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:14.200
 would even go around and like, you know, get bagels from the dumpster at night and, you know,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.540
 scavenge for food. And my parents were kind of like into it. Like they weren't ever like, oh, this is

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.000
 weird. What are you doing? Like they would kind of assign me or my brothers to like go do that. And

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.290
 then we'd like give it out or like have bagels frozen. I mean, we're from Long Island. And so bagels

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:36.330
 is like the main part of the food pyramid, I think bagels and pizza, that's like all people eat. Um,

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:45.010
 and so I, I appreciate things like that. Like I, I appreciate sort of, I don't think I like wax poetic

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:51.440
 about any particular things they made, like, or necessarily feel ever compelled to recreate them,

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.730
 but I appreciate like their work ethic and their resourcefulness and not wanting to waste anything.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:02.830
 Like even my parents came out here and visited once and we were harvesting beans and poor Will was in

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:07.350
 the field and a bean had fallen out of his bag. My dad like picked up the bean and like tapped him

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:11.470
 on the shoulder and was like, there's one thing you should know about us, Will. It's we don't like

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:17.690
 waste. Like terrified the whole rest of the day of this like weird man, like following him around,

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:23.630
 pointing out every mistake. But so I think more than particular kind of food, it was an attitude

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:30.250
 towards it and the value of it. And, um, just knowing that it was like important. Yeah.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:37.090
 So you, um, you got involved in food, not bombs. I did too in Seattle. So, uh, um, you start cooking

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.250
 big quantities of things.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.710
 Yeah. A lot of vegetable oil.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:47.970
 I just like a lot of weird things that again, I don't think I'm ever like, Oh, I should make that

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:55.570
 again. I think anyone should eat, but, um, yeah, it definitely informed a lot of just like interesting

00:05:55.570 --> 00:06:01.670
 thinking and yeah, it was pretty significant to be like an adolescent and left like an imprint more

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:03.610
 of the sentiment about it. So.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:11.860
 And for you, which, um, did you start getting interested in farming first or did you start

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:17.360
 getting interested in, in Korean food and all these crazy varieties of, of plants and animals?

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.740
 Uh, farming first. Yeah.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:26.280
 Yeah. Uh, I always liked gardening. Like my grandparents, um, let us put in a garden at

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:32.400
 their house and, um, just have a lot of memories as a kid of doing that and really liking it.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:40.710
 And then, um, yeah, again with, you know, through food, not bombs just started hanging out around

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:46.560
 like a lot of the community gardens. It was just like really kind of integrated in the, in

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.200
 the locale of like the part of New York where I was spending a lot of time as a teenager.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:58.290
 And so, um, yeah. And I think that although again, like I didn't learn necessarily a lot

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:04.210
 of just like hard science or practical things about gardening, like it made a big impression

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:10.250
 about just the role that space is like that play in connecting people, the way that food

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:15.050
 connects people being in such a diverse place like New York, you just hear people exchanging

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:21.390
 stories about, you know, like food and recipes. And it's just so heartfelt, um, that it just

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:26.910
 felt so compelling. It wasn't like so cerebral as other things. Um, and at that time, like

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:33.440
 it was the areas where I was spending time were, you know, being really impacted by lots

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.500
 of things like Giuliani was the mayor through all of my adolescence. And, um, you know,

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:43.060
 he came down hard on a lot of those art spaces and a lot of those gardens. And when I just

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:47.900
 saw the way people responded, the, that they were just like completely unwilling to like,

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:53.930
 let that go without a fight. It spoke to me about like what, why people would just really

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.450
 dig their heels in like that.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:02.090
 When did you start gardening yourself or when did you start farming?

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:07.690
 Uh, that happened more when I moved to, uh, Washington state. So I lived in Olympia for

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:12.280
 like eight years when I was younger and then got to, I mean, that place is just like full

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:17.500
 of both small scale organic farms and just people that like grow their own food. It's just

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:22.660
 like a basic skill that if you live in Olympia, you're going to be in like probably a weird

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.800
 band at some point and you're going to learn how to grow your own food. It's just like,

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:30.680
 you can't really escape without doing probably both of those things, but at least one of those

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.440
 things. So, yeah.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:35.740
 And you're, and you were at Evergreen or was it?

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.680
 I did sort of, I mean, I went to Evergreen the way that most people did. I took one class

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:46.760
 and then a teacher there had just sponsored me to do contracts. Um, but that was more in

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:54.140
 the realm of like art. I had gone to a state school in New York, uh, FIT studying illustration.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:01.570
 And then, um, when I moved, of course, Evergreen would like take those weird, really specific

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:04.030
 credits. So I just kept on doing art there.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:11.290
 Did you, how did you learn farming? How did you just, you picked it up by osmosis in Olympia?

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:12.570
 Did you work on a farm?

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:18.510
 I mean, I think I, yeah, maybe I should credit Olympia osmosis for some of that knowledge. Um,

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:24.330
 but I, you know, most of the time I've worked, it was in some element of food service or as just

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:31.820
 like a laborer on a farm. And so, um, yeah, for the most part, it was just kind of coming into it

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:37.120
 because I had the opportunity to, and then like learning how much I did actually like the work of it

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:44.060
 and then wanting to learn more. Um, so definitely like kept up some small gardens. Like I, um,

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:52.680
 worked on a friend's small organic farm, uh, outside of Olympia and, um, yeah, so gained some

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:59.240
 practical skills of just like the pace and the manual labor aspect, I suppose. And then, um,

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:06.390
 probably a couple of years before I moved here, maybe like, yeah, 10 years ago or so now, um,

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:13.660
 just had different questions about how we were growing food. And even though my experience was

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:20.480
 solely in organic food production, um, just felt like there were just certain things that I didn't

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:28.470
 know in terms of how sustainable that could truly be. Um, given lots of shifting things,

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:34.970
 both with climate change and just with knowing that, uh, yeah, just certain things revolving

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:40.130
 around other resources and their availability to me. Like I started to question that, or if I wanted

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:45.510
 to keep investing and assuming that I could have access to, you know, oil and other things all of the

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:52.580
 time. And so decided to try to just study as much as I could on my own. And that's where kind of being

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:57.320
 in proximity to Evergreen was really helpful because they have a good sustainable ag program.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:04.730
 And so I just went to their school bookstore and saw what their, um, what their classes were kind of

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:11.590
 learning and then got those books from the library. So just spent a couple years just like buckling down

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:16.710
 and like reading and trying to teach myself soil biology, um, and a little bit of just basic plant

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:24.260
 biology and botany. Um, and so it was just like a funny sort of self-directed, like just reading a lot.

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:29.230
 I have like all these old notebooks where I'm just like transcribing like weird textbooks and trying

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:37.170
 to learn, uh, that way. Yeah. Okay. Yeah. And, um, when did you, when, or when did you first start

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:44.370
 farming when you first started doing it for more than just a garden? Oh, um, so when I moved to

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:51.300
 California, I continued to just work on farms just as basically a farm hand and not having like a lot

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:58.170
 of, you know, like room to express like my own opinions about anything. So that's fine. Um, and then

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:03.970
 I managed to farm in Bolinas and that was the first time where, um, you know, the owner Dennis at

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:09.550
 Paradise Valley, he, he was coming from a similar place. Like when I remember interviewing there and

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:14.630
 he made some reference, I understood with reference to Korean natural farming and he was like a real

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:20.630
 early California organics guy. And I was like, wait, are you talking about master Cho and Korean

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:25.330
 natural farming? He's like, yeah. Like, how do you know about this? It's like, well, I'm Korean.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:29.930
 This is like kind of my birthright. You're just like an old hippie white guy. How do you know

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:35.690
 about this? Like, oh, I saw this other teacher who's popular on Hawaii, like did a series of

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:40.790
 classes in California. And I just really fell in love with it. I was like, oh, I guess I have to

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:46.130
 work here. I was like, no one else has ever kind of mentioned this. And at that point it was just

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:51.250
 still very nascent as an idea and something I wanted to explore. And so the fact that he was

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:57.500
 looking to incorporate more of that into his farm, uh, gave me the opportunity. We just collaborated a lot

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:02.320
 and he gave me like a little lab, like, which was built off of our walk-in where he just like

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:07.380
 basically put a desk in there with a microscope and a little light and a notebook. And he just like

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:13.130
 let me test a lot of stuff. Um, but I think the fact that he, he wanted his farm to kind of revolve

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:18.830
 around those same principles and really believed in it ideologically, uh, just gave me a lot of room

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:26.470
 before starting my own project to, to practice and to kind of refine some of, um, yeah, taking things

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:32.290
 from ideas and translating it to the California landscape. Cause adapting styles of farming that

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:38.920
 are originating in East Asia is like very different here. Um, so yeah, he's the one that gave me the

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:39.440
 opportunity.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:47.640
 So I, I think I realized I learned that Korean food was awesome or like, um, or tasted it for the first

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:53.000
 time when David Chang, like most probably non-Koreans, like David Chang says it's awesome. So I think I'll taste

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:58.460
 Korean food. Yeah. And he, he was, I mean, he was very right. It's really, it's great. Um,

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:05.310
 and so there's kind of a Korean food craze. I mean, as there should be, it's like a, it's amazing food.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:11.000
 But, um, how did that, when did you, when did you start thinking about Korean food?

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:17.180
 Well, I hadn't even eaten Korean food until I had lived in Washington. So I was probably like 19 years

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:23.340
 old and someone was like, there is a pretty significant Korean population like around Olympia.

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:28.920
 So not in Olympia proper, but in like Tanglewild and Lacey, there's a big, there's a Korean market.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:37.200
 Um, and so, you know, tried Korean food then and was interested. And, and I think I just liked

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:45.160
 learning about Korean food, um, just because I am not super outgoing. And so I never really,

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:50.090
 I think never wanted the vulnerability of like seeking out a lot of Korean community,

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:56.810
 having not grown up around it and feeling a little apart from it. Um, so learning about food was just

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:01.470
 something I could do on my own. And I wanted to sort of figure out what it meant to me,

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:09.060
 maybe a little bit in isolation because I didn't want it to be so, um, impacted by other people,

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:14.520
 like an external validation that could or could not come. Um, and then I think,

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:20.670
 yeah, just in general, kind of really liking plants since I was younger. And even like my mom jokes

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:26.420
 around about like, my youngest brother really loves animals. She's like, you're Kevin likes

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:31.080
 animals more than people. You always like plants more than people. She's like, I've only got one

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:36.100
 sort of socially well-adjusted child. That's my middle brother. She's like the other two of you

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:42.100
 on either end, just like mostly prefer the company of these other things to humans. Uh, and my middle

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:50.120
 brother is really socially graceful and friendly. Um, so I think that with plants, it just made sense

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:55.700
 because there is like a type of connection you can have and something that mirrors something back to

00:15:55.700 --> 00:16:02.420
 you. And I think that I do sort of have the sense that, you know, plants can act as this placeholder

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:09.450
 and almost like a teacher, um, in a different way. And their sort of knowledge, you know, goes back so

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:16.760
 much longer than what I could learn from, uh, another person or yet. Or if I even had access to other

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:22.310
 people that wanted to share that knowledge at that point. Um, so I think through studying, like I really

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:28.520
 like studying like the history of plants, the co-evolution of plants and people. Um, and so that just

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:34.820
 has given me what I feel like at this point is like a little more solid footing of, of my relationship

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:43.840
 to having been born in Korea, my identity as a Korean American. Um, and yeah, I feel like there is

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:49.960
 just such a sweet relationship you can have with plants and, and just kind of, yeah, what they open up

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:50.680
 for you.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:56.200
 I was reading around the internet preparing and, and I read something about the shiso, the Korean

00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:03.910
 perilla was, um, kind of a spark of fascination with vegetables. Like, well, uh, how did you find

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:09.430
 that vegetable or is it, is it an herb or a vegetable? And how did you find that? What was that like?

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:17.450
 Uh, I found it just in a seed catalog basically, and I didn't know anything about it, but I, I did want

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:22.970
 to grow something that was distinctly Korean. And so it was kind of like looking at my options and had a

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:28.810
 small garden. And then, um, yeah, just saw a listing for it and then cross-referenced it with

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:32.450
 different cookbooks. I was like, Oh, it does seem like this is pretty popular. It's an herb, so it

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:39.360
 should be fairly straightforward. And so grew it. And I do just sort of like, yeah, feel nostalgic

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:43.520
 looking back where I'm like, Oh, I didn't even know like what the cotyledons look like. I didn't

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:48.360
 know what it was going to look like when it germinated. And, and just the way you have any sort of like

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:53.600
 great love you think about just the time that you invest and, and how well you get to

00:17:53.600 --> 00:18:01.270
 know something, um, just through experience and years of, uh, repetition. Um, and so it

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:06.070
 was just something where it is just like a really beautiful plant. And so right away just

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:12.210
 was really interesting, um, and liked growing it on my own. And then I did feel like it was

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:17.750
 something that when other Korean people would see it, or if I brought it somewhere, it's just

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:25.410
 such a quick, uh, point of reference for people to connect on because it's so ubiquitous. And, um,

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:31.210
 and so I think, yeah, it just, I do credit it with like leading me to all these directions where it

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:36.310
 was like something that started very solitary in a way that was comfortable to me with my temperament,

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:43.210
 uh, ended up introducing me and, and bringing me into the fold in terms of having like a really robust,

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:49.420
 really special Korean community in the Bay. Um, and I feel like, yeah, that plant kind of like

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:54.260
 acted as like, you know, the older wizard, like ushering me into that in this certain way.

00:18:54.620 --> 00:19:00.290
 And so, uh, yeah, so that was like the first thing and it still is like a really indelible. It's like

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:06.860
 another thing that because it's day length sensitive, you know, someone recently was just talking about

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:11.140
 like moving to Hawaii and I was like, well, I have to find out if Perilla will grow there because it

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:17.300
 might be too day neutral and blah, blah, blah. And so it does sort of, yeah, it will dictate a certain

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:19.920
 thing, just like having a certain devotion to it.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.930
 And, um, and how did you end up farming here in Sunil?

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:32.170
 Well, when I first moved here, I was interested in volunteering for, uh, people's grocery and they

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:38.190
 had a plot here. Um, and so like two days after I moved here, I just came out and volunteered.

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:45.270
 And on that day there was like a neighboring farmer who just needed help. Um, and so I went

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:49.490
 and helped him and then he offered me a job. And so then I just started working here pretty

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:57.220
 much. Um, and so pretty much from the moment I moved to California, apart from the year I

00:19:57.220 --> 00:20:02.020
 lived in Bolinas, I've been at the ag park in some capacity. So when I moved back from Bolinas,

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:07.380
 I approached Fred, the person I had worked for, and, uh, he had asked me to come back to work

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:11.280
 and I said like, Oh, I have kind of an idea. I want to do like a side project. I'd started

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:18.230
 growing some Korean crops in Bolinas and, uh, had been introduced to the brothers who own

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:24.390
 Namu during that time. I brought them Perilla. And, uh, so it told Fred like, Oh, if I come

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:29.350
 back to work, could we work out something where I could sublease like a certain amount of space

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:35.190
 on your farm? I want to try growing some stuff for restaurants and specialty markets. He said,

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:39.870
 fine. And then it ended up that this acre just opened up. And so we just started subleasing

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:40.510
 from him.

00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:43.040
 Do you sell everything to Namu Gaji?

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:49.140
 Yeah. So in the beginning, uh, Namu had approached me about growing those chili peppers.

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:54.740
 And then when this sort of opened up, originally I was going to work at Fred's and kind of was like

00:20:54.740 --> 00:21:01.150
 going to grow, you know, 10 rows of whatever I wanted. Uh, but when this opened up, talked to

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:05.290
 Dennis and was like, Oh, we had this opportunity. Should we, you know, and he wanted to start

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:10.330
 a separate farm. And so we just decided to collaborate on that. So they invested the money

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:17.720
 for us to lease it. So they paid the rent and the water. And then I spent that first year just sort

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:24.820
 of like, uh, trying to see if we could get it operable. And they were really excited, which was

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:30.700
 unique about the idea of the way that I wanted to farm using those natural farming methods. And so

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:36.000
 in the beginning, it was all an experiment. They invested sort of the capital to start and I invested

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:43.380
 the labor with my friend Peter and, um, yeah, so they took a little bit of a risk because they had

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:48.420
 said like, Oh, let's do a small garden for the restaurant. And I was like, well, I want to do

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:54.230
 with like no fossil fuel. So that means like no mechanization. And it also means like, I don't

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:59.930
 want things that have to be like, you know, industrially extracted from other parts of the world.

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:04.870
 Like I want all our fertility to be like self-generated. They're like, all right, you know,

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:11.960
 like kind of believed in it ideologically. Um, and so I think, yeah, I just feel really fortunate.

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:17.660
 It's like, it was just the right relationship because certainly, you know, in our first couple

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:22.780
 years, especially there were just so many ups and downs with like calibrating this plot to

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:29.300
 that system, like being so different in terms of nutrient management and, um, yeah. And wanting

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:34.720
 to invest in long-term solutions and not problem solving anything with a short-term fix, like

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:41.320
 means you just had real variation in terms of like, something's not working. Like when we would have

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:48.610
 insects or disease, anything, we approached it with like that perspective of like, let's let things

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:54.590
 do their thing. And if, if we lose over half of the crop, we have to just let it do that. And then

00:22:54.590 --> 00:23:01.260
 we're going to like save seed and select for resilience. And so I think having it, that just

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:04.960
 couldn't have happened if they didn't like believe in what we were working towards, like if they had

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:12.500
 to make everything pencil out row by row as a commodity, like it would just be too hard. And

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:19.680
 that's why you have farmers that just are in that tough position to have to make, make the short-term

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:25.940
 work. And we just had the luxury and privilege to not have to do that. So we could just like

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:30.940
 approach it from like how seed breeders look at it. Like if you don't kill half your crop,

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:34.480
 you're not getting any good data about what is actually resistant.

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:41.850
 I heard that from, from, well, not, not commercial seed breeders, but some kind of renegade seed

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:46.470
 breeders. You want to start with the ones that are vulnerable and that's how you can build multiple

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:51.370
 resistance, not just single trait resistance. How many, how many years have you been saving some of

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:58.460
 these seeds? Uh, pretty much the whole duration of the farm. So because we're growing such particular

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:06.830
 things and because it, it came a lot from Dennis, uh, Dennis Lee, his families, uh, they gave us

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:13.000
 different seeds to start. So like the mustards, the chilies, the perilla, um, we just knew we had to

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:19.960
 save our own seeds in the beginning and then, uh, started refining that as we went. And then even just

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:25.440
 after the first year, we hosted like a Korean harvest festival for the holiday Chuseok, which is like

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:31.960
 Korean Thanksgiving. And all these folks came cause we wanted to have this style of, uh, traditional

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:39.530
 drumming that always accompanies agriculture. And so, um, we ended up just having tons of folks that I had

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:49.270
 no idea who they were come out. And then, um, you know, people just responded so quickly to see like

00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:55.150
 something that was really explicitly about Korean food. And so suddenly people were just like giving

00:24:55.150 --> 00:25:00.850
 me seeds that were like from their family or like, yeah, seeds that they wanted, like a little bit

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:05.170
 of access to. So like, Oh, you could grow this. Can you just give me a little? And so it just sort of

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:08.970
 evolved from there where suddenly we became this clearing house for people to just like send us

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:18.150
 seeds for, um, because, you know, we had a lot of, especially younger families like approaching us

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:22.940
 because they really believe in sort of like the food politics of like somewhere like the Bay

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:30.360
 were limited in having access to like Korean vegetables grown organically. And so they were

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:36.230
 constantly having to like choose and prioritize, like, do we want to like omit these really significant

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:40.850
 things and these like things we want our kids to grow up eating or do we want to prioritize like

00:25:40.850 --> 00:25:44.050
 general health and wellbeing? So.

00:25:44.860 --> 00:25:50.380
 Right. Um, Chelsea, I have a friend who's an anthropologist. Her name is Anna Singh. And she,

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:57.850
 um, she, I've heard her a few times talk to young farmers and be like, you got to grow some Asian

00:25:57.850 --> 00:26:03.470
 vegetables. I can't stand it. Like I can't find organic Asian vegetables anywhere. Um, and then

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:07.570
 everyone's like, yeah, that's a good, I just don't know if people would buy them. Is that,

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:15.110
 is that true? What do you, I mean, I don't know what, what makes that, uh, yeah. What makes that

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:17.800
 difficult to find organic Asian vegetables?

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:23.660
 Well, I mean, I know for me, like I work for an older Korean lady on the weekends who has a mushroom

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:30.400
 farm. Um, and it is just like, cause I would bring her radishes and stuff in the beginning. She'd be

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:34.160
 like, how much would you charge for this? And I would tell her, you know, honestly, we charged

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:38.870
 probably $2 a pound. She's like, oh, you know, at the Korean market, it's this much. And

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:44.960
 so I think it's, I don't think it's unique to Asian vegetables. I think it's just indicative

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:50.950
 of like breaking down like the true costs and the true value of food in general. Um, and

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:56.740
 people's perception and food access. Um, cause I think people ask us a lot about that as well.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:27:01.800
 Like, oh, like long beans, for example. Oh, you grow long beans, super stoked on that.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:05.860
 But when you go to like the old Oakland farmer's market in the summer, that's like,

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:11.850
 it's long beans at every stall. Cause it's all the sort of, uh, Asian farmers coming from different

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:18.100
 parts of the Valley. And so to me, I think like we're in a unique position because we can, uh,

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:23.260
 yeah, we can leverage our position and we're just articulating so much the importance of organics,

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:28.840
 but we're kind of within the organic field, growing Asian vegetables. And I'd like for us to someday be in a

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:35.960
 position like to work so that those older Asian farmers don't get kind of locked out of like that higher end

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:43.500
 market. Like we try to brainstorm, not just having more young farmers growing Asian vegetables, but to

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:51.520
 question like, yeah, what are the barriers in perception or the marketplace that dictate to farmers that lower

00:27:51.520 --> 00:28:00.300
 value and then try to address that. And if we can raise the value of that in people's sort of frame of reference,

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:06.820
 then can other farmers like have the incentives they need to kind of grow organic and get a better price for it.

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:14.670
 So we've tried to break down what to make a case for in terms of that. Um, and I think it is like a lot of good

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:21.230
 questions, like similarly to like all the dialogue that happens about, um, you know, culturally specific foods and like

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:28.950
 authenticity, all of these things. Um, you know, the USDA has allotted more and more money through the years to specialty crop

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:37.240
 production, including Asian vegetables. And so I think that even amongst other peers, like have the sense that Asian

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:45.000
 vegetables are seen as something that is a pretty decent margin. And if you can get it dialed in, uh, there is a niche

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:52.180
 market for it, especially with within the restaurant industry. Um, so I think it is sort of emerging. And my question is

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:58.880
 more like what will make that economy as it emerges, like the most equitable for, for people who have grown

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:04.660
 it traditionally or, uh, for immigrant and refugee farmers, like who may be at different points were told

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:10.500
 to grow different higher grossing commodity crops, like strawberries or something like what would allow

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:18.730
 them to grow the food that maybe they do care for intending their own stories. Um, simultaneous to like

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:26.250
 it providing a good anchor for newer and beginning farmers to kind of like have, uh, yeah, better margin

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:33.940
 on something like, um, you know, like an interesting sort of mustard green versus like a standard salad

00:29:33.940 --> 00:29:41.880
 green or something like that. And you've been working with Kitazawa seed company, right? And, um, yeah,

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:47.040
 can you tell me about that collaboration? Yeah. So we do their field trials for them. And I was like,

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:53.910
 you know, such an avid customer of Kitazawa for so long. And they, of course, like played a huge role in being

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:59.830
 able to start this farm and having access, like, I would just scan their catalog. And if anything, mentioned it was

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:06.470
 Korean, I just bought it, you know, like figure out if we can grow it later. We'll try all these experiments. But, um,

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:13.030
 yeah, and I think at one point, I was like, had been ordering for them from them for a couple years. And it was still

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:18.550
 like, yeah, fairly in the beginning of the farm. And finally, I called in one day and was like, Oh,

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:25.200
 can I pick it up? I'm in Oakland, their offices in Oakland. And finally, uh, Maya, the owner had just

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:29.180
 said, like, you know, I've been seeing your name on all these invoices. And she's like, I couldn't

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:36.040
 figure out like, based on your name, which is not very Asian sounding, why you're buying so much Asian

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:40.840
 crops. She's like, and then I saw a newspaper article, and I realized you are Asian. She's like,

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:45.500
 so now I got to meet you. So come up to the office. And so through that funny interaction,

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:54.950
 um, yeah, just started a friendship, basically. Uh, it was demystified. Yes, I am Asian. Um,

00:30:55.330 --> 00:31:01.450
 and so yeah, through that friendship, and just like, them learning more about my farm, and me learning

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:08.730
 more about just, like, they both, uh, Maya and Jim, who own it, come from like, really rich agricultural

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:16.020
 legacies in California. Um, and so just, they became really strong mentors and really close

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:20.980
 friends. Um, and they really just started to believe in like, some of the data we were collecting

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:30.280
 about yet drought, and sort of climate change resistant farming methods. Um, and so they have

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:35.260
 just like, tried to, I, you know, for them, I think in a lot of ways, they've just tried to find ways to

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:41.570
 support me, um, because they believe in this. And then, uh, being their trial farm has been

00:31:41.570 --> 00:31:47.260
 interesting, because we just get to try a lot of seeds and try to track. And with working with the

00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:52.160
 restaurant, it works really well to have that sort of level of integration, because we could send it to

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:58.470
 the cooks and at Namu, and they can give us feedback that then gets filtered into like, yeah, just what that

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:06.330
 looks like practically for them to either purchase or not purchase different new varieties. Um, and so

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:10.090
 it's been a nice partnership. And I know that sometimes they even get calls of like, oh, I had

00:32:10.090 --> 00:32:15.510
 a turnip at dinner at Namu. Like, what turnip is it that they're growing, you know, and sending to the

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:22.610
 restaurant. Um, and so it's been pretty fun, and it's been a really good learning experience. And we get

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:28.530
 to just try so much stuff. And we get to really see like, because a lot of the Asian seed industry is

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:35.410
 so geared towards hybrid production right now. Um, you know, it's been interesting to just see like

00:32:35.410 --> 00:32:39.970
 the access to different Korean varieties of crops that are open pollinated, what's there and what's

00:32:39.970 --> 00:32:48.300
 not, why are those things represented, uh, or not represented. And, um, so it's been interesting to

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:54.420
 have that sort of be able to perceive all of those things from within that industry. And we just get

00:32:54.420 --> 00:32:59.940
 to see like how hybrids factor into kind of this style of farming, which is more akin to, you know,

00:33:00.040 --> 00:33:05.430
 peasant subsistence farming, essentially. Um, so just all the criticisms you hear about the green

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:10.830
 revolution about like what we're exporting in with these sort of like whole packages of agricultural

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:17.050
 production. It's you buy the seed, you buy the fertility, um, and just how precarious and dangerous

00:33:17.050 --> 00:33:23.850
 that is. Like we do get to see sort of those seeds factored into a system that's not suited for that.

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:29.690
 And like what performs well and what doesn't. And, and just kind of will, we can put like a hybrid

00:33:29.690 --> 00:33:35.330
 variety next to like maybe an open pollinated variety or something we've saved. And at this point,

00:33:35.390 --> 00:33:39.730
 I feel like we can make a case for just like, maybe not on a commercial industrial scale,

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:46.410
 but like for small scale farmers, like it truly does behoove you to, to have your own sort of seed

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:47.930
 saving program and regimen.

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:57.670
 Seems like, like, um, a lot of really good farmers I've met. You're, you're constantly experimenting

00:33:57.670 --> 00:34:01.730
 with things. Well, what are some of the things that you're experimenting with now or thinking

00:34:01.730 --> 00:34:05.890
 about for next season to kind of, to, um, to tinker with or to kind of push forward?

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:10.150
 Well, a lot of it is like mostly self-serving in that we've been,

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:14.250
 the lifespan of the farm has really coincided with the worsening of the drought.

00:34:14.590 --> 00:34:19.850
 And we've seen interesting information just in terms of like, we're using less and less

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:24.170
 water each year, but our production has gone continuously up and we're planting more intensively

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:29.310
 than ever. And I think a lot of the credit for that, along with kind of the soil building

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:38.650
 practices is a lot in seed selection and, uh, and being thoughtful around that. Um, and so some of it,

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:45.270
 just most of it is geared towards, yeah, just general growing with less, with less water and

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:52.500
 minimal supplemental fertility. Um, so we'll like, so for eggplant, if we grow four rows of

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:58.420
 eggplant, we'll grow two for production and then two just for, like we were talking about seed saving

00:34:58.420 --> 00:35:04.750
 and like kind of subject that to a little bit gnarlier conditions. Like you have like the ones you coddle a

00:35:04.750 --> 00:35:12.470
 little bit and then the ones you, you know, kind of exposed to more stress in, in a somewhat thoughtful

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:17.430
 fashion. Um, so after a couple of seasons of doing that, like we do have our own sort of really

00:35:17.430 --> 00:35:24.340
 vigorous seed stock that, um, is just really tailored to our production. And so we can, um, scale back

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:30.480
 how much we do have to like tend to those things. But we also have had the opportunity to collaborate

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:37.870
 with other, you know, folks within Organic Seed Alliance who's doing a lot to try to, um, yeah,

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:45.410
 really bolster participatory seed breeding for organic production. Um, and so Steve Peters,

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:50.510
 who's the regional coordinator for the Central Coast has come out here and we've tried doing a,

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:57.060
 uh, broccoli project for him where we grew like 200 broccoli plants for the summer in Sunal,

00:35:57.280 --> 00:36:02.360
 which is basically like putting something in like its own version of hell on earth. And, uh, yeah,

00:36:02.360 --> 00:36:08.340
 he was the one who told me like, well, if you get two to produce seed, you'll be successful. Like,

00:36:08.340 --> 00:36:13.240
 oh my God, you're just like walking all these little starts off to like a death march. Like this is

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:21.170
 kind of morbid. Um, but yeah, I think just from gleaning some of his knowledge and, and he's a really

00:36:21.170 --> 00:36:27.820
 good teacher, um, has been helpful to kind of formalize it a little bit more because before it was just

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:32.870
 more a matter of like our weird style of farming, we just knew we needed seed that was like going to

00:36:32.870 --> 00:36:39.300
 be able to adapt and show some resilience to growing with less. So similar to like, yeah,

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:46.270
 old land races. And, um, and now I think we can think a little bit broader vision, a little bit

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:52.710
 more about wanting to provide like some more access to open pollinated Asian vegetables than,

00:36:52.790 --> 00:36:58.250
 than is currently there. And so Kitazawa has supported me in wanting to start like a seed line. So we have

00:36:58.250 --> 00:37:04.230
 our own seed line with them called like second generation seed line. And, uh, that will focus

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:12.340
 on all like heirloom open pollinated varieties. Oh my gosh. Um, so many questions. Um, I guess my

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:19.360
 biggest question is what sort of, and drove you to seek out relationships with restaurants as opposed

00:37:19.360 --> 00:37:28.120
 to doing a more like farmer's market style production. Um, well, when I moved here, I really do look back and

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:33.630
 think I realized like, Oh, I was such like a little country bumpkin. Cause I think even before I moved

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:41.220
 to California, um, just started getting more and more interested in like restaurants and food. Um,

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:48.360
 and had seen so much of the growing aspect of it, but Olympia, um, doesn't have the same sort of like

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:54.740
 restaurant economy and like cultural sort of cachet that it does here. So I remember even a friend at the

00:37:54.740 --> 00:38:01.520
 library there, um, putting some books on hold for me from time to time about like, yeah, cookbooks and

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:07.200
 food. And she put a book about Chez Panisse in my hold account. And I read it. And I remember being on

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:12.910
 the farm I was at reading it and there's this place called Chez Panisse and they buy direct from farms.

00:38:12.910 --> 00:38:18.350
 Like maybe someday I'll sell vegetables to them. And the person I was working with was like, you know,

00:38:18.390 --> 00:38:22.710
 that's like the most famous restaurant in the world. Right. And I was like, Oh, really? He's like,

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:28.360
 yeah. Like quit dreaming about this. Like, that's insane. Like that's like the most famous restaurant

00:38:28.360 --> 00:38:33.840
 in the world right now. He's like, yeah, right. You're going to sell vegetables to Chez Panisse.

00:38:33.900 --> 00:38:39.690
 And I just still remember being like, Oh wow. I had just no concept about the rich history here.

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:44.670
 So when I moved here, it was like just kind of exciting. And I feel like I learned a lot. Um,

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:53.590
 you know, when I worked for Fred at Bayonikia, that was a big part of his business was, uh,

00:38:53.590 --> 00:39:02.110
 kind of like, yeah, interesting specialty crops basically. Um, and so I saw a lot of facets,

00:39:02.780 --> 00:39:07.500
 facets of it and things that I liked and things that I didn't like about just like why edible flowers

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:13.960
 are like so much more expensive than like a bunch of carrots or something that is actual sustenance.

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:20.470
 Um, but I do think that, yeah, partially just fueled by excitement and just like how creative

00:39:20.470 --> 00:39:26.090
 a lot of the people that I got to meet within the restaurant industry were. Uh, I also worked at

00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:32.890
 Camino restaurant when I first moved here as a cook and Russ, um, Russ, the owner and chef of that

00:39:32.890 --> 00:39:38.530
 restaurant. Like I just learned so much from him and getting to see it from that side from people that

00:39:38.530 --> 00:39:45.850
 really just like, I, I really do appreciate him specifically because he has such integrity with the

00:39:45.850 --> 00:39:52.320
 way he thinks about food. Um, and a lot of our philosophy is like, I felt, uh, just a lot of

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:57.020
 resonance basically. Like a lot of the way I run this farm feels similar to like the ideals he

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:03.440
 practices at Camino. And, um, so I think getting to see it from that and made it feel like something

00:40:03.440 --> 00:40:07.860
 more interesting to just see things transformed or to see it in the hands of people that really

00:40:07.940 --> 00:40:13.680
 respected it. Um, and, um, and in terms of just sheer practicality, like a lot of the stuff that

00:40:13.680 --> 00:40:20.870
 I did want to grow, um, even thinking about selling it in a place where there is a ton of Korean

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:26.210
 American community, but not having, you know, a ton of just credibility on my own as an individual,

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:33.420
 like, um, growing for NAMU made a lot of sense because they were really enthusiastic about like

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:39.160
 those longer backstories and wanting heirlooms and not just wanting the like really glossy, uh,

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:43.950
 versions of things that are commercially available now. And so it's been like an interesting dialogue

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:49.670
 constantly through the years of just, um, yeah, what varieties are available at somewhere like

00:40:49.670 --> 00:40:54.230
 Coriana Plaza and why are those the things that are available? And a lot of the stuff that we're

00:40:54.230 --> 00:41:01.450
 growing predates some of that. And so, um, it may taste different, it may look different. Um,

00:41:02.090 --> 00:41:06.780
 but the partnership with NAMU has allowed us to like have those conversations in a way that people

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:11.340
 just sort of trust or kind of look up to because NAMU is such a good restaurant. Um,

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:17.960
 and so it just gave us a lot of opportunity to, um, expedite the process where we weren't

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:23.020
 introducing things that were like maybe somewhat familiar, but also somewhat foreign in a regular

00:41:23.020 --> 00:41:30.660
 marketplace and let people sort of like comfort with it build slowly. Like I know a lot of, uh,

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:36.360
 other farmers like Annabelle Littering, whose farm La Tercera is like out of Star Route Farm and in

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:42.000
 Petaluma. Um, she brought a lot of like interesting, uh, Italian heirlooms here,

00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:47.560
 you know, decades ago. And she just talks about like being so committed to them because she was

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:53.920
 really like kind of rigorous in her study of Italian food and like finding interesting seeds,

00:41:53.920 --> 00:41:58.860
 but just that it took so much time for that to build before even something we take for granted,

00:41:58.940 --> 00:42:03.520
 like Lassenado Kale, you know, like it had a period where it was like a kind of a hard sell at a market.

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:09.740
 Um, so just knowing the resources I had in terms of like capital, the ability to kind of like be in

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:16.600
 the red as a business for how long, um, just knew that like, I did want to grow things that felt like

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:21.950
 I believed in them and I cared about them and I didn't have like a ton of time to just sort of like

00:42:21.950 --> 00:42:27.770
 let it sit at a market and not sell or try to like constantly be pitching it to people. Um,

00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:33.580
 and with the restaurant too, they can, um, they can make so many value added products and things that

00:42:33.580 --> 00:42:39.120
 are just smart for any small scale farmer to think about, like in terms of converting potential waste or

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:44.320
 things that are seconds or calls at the market. Like I do think even if we didn't have this exclusive

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:51.030
 relationship, restaurants would comprise a pretty big part of it just because, um, it streamlines a

00:42:51.030 --> 00:42:57.700
 lot of the processes on this, on a small farm. Great. Um, yeah, I think the only other question

00:42:57.700 --> 00:43:02.140
 that I always love asking people what they enjoy cooking, um, cause it's something I'm really

00:43:02.140 --> 00:43:07.280
 passionate about as well. So I know you said you're pretty busy now, but I have a, you know,

00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:12.340
 you said December 1st, maybe that glorious day when it comes and you can cook yourself something

00:43:12.340 --> 00:43:22.520
 for dinner. Uh, what, what do you think? That's a good question. Um, I really, I do really enjoy

00:43:22.520 --> 00:43:28.680
 cooking and I feel like I'm surrounded by a lot of creative people and we like cooking together. Um,

00:43:29.420 --> 00:43:33.800
 I really like pretty simple food though. Honestly, like I'm just like, Oh, if I just have like a

00:43:33.800 --> 00:43:40.420
 little rice, I like eating pickles. Um, I really in the winter, like making, uh, you know, like

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:47.520
 juke, like rice porridge. Um, so like experimenting with different kinds of that. Um, so that's maybe

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:52.370
 like one of the comfort foods I look forward to, like slowing down and then being able to eat all

00:43:52.370 --> 00:43:59.590
 through the winter, like just a nice little bowl of porridge. Awesome. Well, thanks so much. I, uh,

00:44:00.090 --> 00:44:04.390
 this has been really great. It's been great to see your farm and to hear the story. So I really

00:44:04.390 --> 00:44:18.640
 appreciate it. No, thank you guys for coming out here. Delicious Revolution is a show about food,

